## Marker Conventions

When extracting or synthesizing content, four marker types are recognized by the audit toolkit. Use them precisely — the score depends on the distinction:

| Marker | Meaning | Audit treatment |
|---|---|---|
| `GAP: [text]` | Action item. Knowledge that should exist here but doesn't. Filling it would resolve the marker. | Scored by Epistemics **E4**, normalised per 100 concept pages. |
| `ACKNOWLEDGED: [text]` | Documented limitation. Information the source material does not contain (e.g. "exact dosing not specified in any source"). Not an action item — a known boundary of the corpus. | Appears in the rollup; not scored against the vault. |
| `CONFLICT: [text]` | Two sources disagree on a fact, unresolved. A downstream bot reading this page will produce a wrong answer. | Scored by Epistemics **E3**. Convert to `ACKNOWLEDGED:` only once the resolution is recorded in `corrections.md` — **one file at a time, after reading it.** |
| `ASSUMPTION: [text]` | A synthesis decision made while bridging sources. Awaiting owner ratification. | Appears in the rollup; not scored. Owner ratifies (remove marker) or corrects (revise text). |

Bulk-converting `CONFLICT:` to `ACKNOWLEDGED:` across the vault raises the Epistemics score while leaving every wrong answer in place. It is the single easiest way to corrupt this vault's usefulness, and the marker distinction exists to make it visible rather than convenient.

**The distinction matters because:** the original failure mode (concepts mentioned across many source pages but missing as wiki pages) was invisible because every limitation was treated as an equal `GAP:`. The four-marker convention names what each one actually is so coverage can be measured accurately.

